  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 148 PM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nMid-level ridging will continue to dominate the period leading to \nwarm and mostly rain free conditions outside of sparse seabreeze \nconvection each day. Early Friday morning, we will see a mid-level \nshortwave tracking along the southern periphery of the ridge \nbeginning to head onshore. This will lead to an increase in the \nmoisture regime, surging PWATs around 2.0 inches along the mid-upper \ncoastline of the CWA, with 2.1\" PWAT values up towards the Victoria \nCrossroads. This shortwave will bring PVA to the area resulting in \nsmall scale lift which will allow for low rain chances (20-30%) \nduring the morning and afternoon on Friday. \n\nWill continue to monitor the need for short-fused heat related \nproducts. We could see peak heat indices of 110 to 115 with dew \npoints in the mid to upper 70s into early next week. This translates \nto moderate to extreme HeatRisk conditions. An additional risk for \nelevated fire weather exists over the Rio Grande Plains into early \nnext week due to low RH (25-30%) and sustained 20mph winds gusting \nto 25mph. We strongly encourage the practice of heat safety in these \nconditions. Take frequent breaks and remember to stay hydrated, \ncheck those backseats, and avoid long exposure in these \ntemperatures. For more information be sure to check out \nweather.gov/heatsafety.",
